      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To explore the clinical application of laparoscopy in gastrointestinal abdominal emergency."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Clinical data of 44 cases with undefined acute abdomen undergoing laparoscopic surgery from October 2008 to October 2011 were analyzed retrospectively. Sixty-five cases treated by regular surgery during the same period were enrolled as controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In laparoscopic surgery group, 42 cases were diagnosed under laparoscopy(95.5%, 42/44). Thirty-four (77.3%,34/44) patients received operation successfully after diagnosis, including 20 of total laparoscopy, 14 of assistant small incision. Compared with control group, laparoscopic group had shorter length of incision[(6.7±2.2) cm vs. (15.8±3.4) cm], less blood loss[(51.4±30.3) ml vs. (117.9±49.5) ml], faster recovery of postoperative gastrointestinal function[postoperative oral intake(15.0±6.1) d vs. (30.5±8.4) d], shorter hospital stay[(5.6±4.2) d vs. (8.4±4.8) d] (all P<0.05), lower complication rate, and less surgical cost(P>0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Laparoscopy is safe and effective in treating gastrointestinal abdominal emergency and therapeutic operation can be performed after a definite diagnosis."
        }
      ]},
